  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nForecast remains on track...\n\nTonight and early morning may see some spots with low stratus and\nfog development but fewer spots than last night. \n\nOverall, the transition away from summer is appearing in the \nsynoptic pattern taking shape over the next week. Upper-level \nridging generally around the southern/central parts of the country\nwill still keep temperatures near to above normal in the broader \nregion, but split flow is noted in the guidance into next week. \nSouthern jet stream around the periphery of the ridge will carry \nembedded shortwaves and add more flow and shear to the atmosphere.\nShortwaves may bring showers or storms around Sunday/Monday, but \nthe models disagree whether ridging or troughing will be more \nestablished to allow much. Either way, the conditions will be \ndrier without concern for humidity causing dangerous heat stress.\n\nBy the time we get to the middle of next week, the ensemble of\nguidance shows a breakdown of the ridge and a more full-latitude \ntrough swinging from the western CONUS toward the central CONUS. \nBeing a bit early in the transitional time of year, confidence on \nthe timing of this features is very low. Official guidance leans \ninto a faster and more progressive low with a cold front and \nstorms in our area by Wednesday. The ECMWF and other guidance \ncontinue to suggest a slower low cutting off around the Desert \nSouthwest and continued warm advection across our forecast area \nand region (rain to the northwest still) through Wednesday. We \nwill definitely have to monitor this potential system, as it marks\na change from the stagnant summer pattern and a potentially more \npotent cool-season pattern. In addition, there will be significant\nsubtropical moisture related to east Pacific tropical cyclone \nactivity.\n\nThat being said, our best chance for the next rain and storms\ncoming through is Wednesday afternoon with the aforementioned\nsystem. Light showers and fog/mist persisting overnight from\nWednesday through the weekend; and continued diurnally driven\nstorms developing in the afternoon. Rainfall does not currently\nlook to be considerable with this system, so any flash flooding\nrisks are close to nil. /OAJ/",
